<subsection xml:id="subsec-quadratic-forms">
    <title>Quadratic Forms</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            A <em>quadratic form</em> is a function that maps a vector to a real number
            using only squared and mixed products of its components — no linear or constant
            terms appear. Quadratic forms arise naturally in many contexts: the kinetic energy
            of a mechanical system, the elastic energy stored in a deformed structure, and
            the local behaviour of a differentiable function near a critical point are all
            described by quadratic forms. The key tool for analysing them is the diagonalization
            of the underlying symmetric matrix.
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <definition xml:id="def-quadratic-form">
        <title>Quadratic Form</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A <term>quadratic form</term> is a map
                <me>
                    q \colon \R^n \to \R,
                    \qquad
                    \mathbf{v} \mapsto \mathbf{v}^\top A \mathbf{v},
                </me>
                where <m>A</m> is a symmetric <m>n \times n</m> matrix.
                The matrix <m>A</m> is called the <term>matrix of the quadratic form</term>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <remark xml:id="rem-symmetrization">
        <p>
            Every quadratic form can be uniquely represented by a symmetric matrix. If a
            non-symmetric matrix <m>M</m> is given, we can replace it by the symmetric matrix
            <me>
                A = \tfrac{1}{2}(M + M^\top)
            </me>
            without changing the quadratic form, since
            <m>\mathbf{v}^\top M \mathbf{v} = \mathbf{v}^\top A \mathbf{v}</m>
            for all <m>\mathbf{v}</m>.
        </p>
    </remark>

    <!-- ADDED: concrete engineering motivation for quadratic forms -->
    <remark>
        <p>
            In engineering, quadratic forms often measure energy. For instance, if
            <m>\mathbf{x}</m> records two small displacements in a coupled spring system,
            then the elastic energy can have the form
            <m>q(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{x}^\top A\mathbf{x}</m>. Positive definiteness then
            means that every non-zero displacement stores positive energy, which is exactly
            what we expect from a stable configuration.
        </p>
    </remark>

    <example xml:id="ex-quadratic-form-2x2">
        <title>Expanding a Quadratic Form</title>
        <p>
            Let
            <me>
                A = \begin{pmatrix} 1 &amp; 2 \\ 2 &amp; 3 \end{pmatrix}.
            </me>
            The associated quadratic form is
            <md>
                <mrow>
                    q_A(\mathbf{x})
                    &amp;= \begin{pmatrix} x_1 &amp; x_2 \end{pmatrix}
                       \begin{pmatrix} 1 &amp; 2 \\ 2 &amp; 3 \end{pmatrix}
                       \begin{pmatrix} x_1 \\ x_2 \end{pmatrix}
                </mrow>
                <mrow>
                    &amp;= \begin{pmatrix} x_1 &amp; x_2 \end{pmatrix}
                       \begin{pmatrix} x_1 + 2x_2 \\ 2x_1 + 3x_2 \end{pmatrix}
                </mrow>
                <mrow>
                    &amp;= x_1^2 + 4x_1 x_2 + 3x_2^2.
                </mrow>
            </md>
            Observe that the coefficient of the mixed term <m>x_1 x_2</m> is
            <m>a_{12} + a_{21} = 2 + 2 = 4</m>.
        </p>
    </example>

    <definition xml:id="def-definiteness">
        <title>Definiteness of a Quadratic Form</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>A</m> be a symmetric <m>n \times n</m> matrix with associated
                quadratic form <m>q(\mathbf{v}) = \mathbf{v}^\top A \mathbf{v}</m>.
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>A</m> is <term>positive definite</term> if
                        <m>q(\mathbf{v}) \gt 0</m> for all <m>\mathbf{v} \neq \mathbf{0}</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>A</m> is <term>positive semidefinite</term> if
                        <m>q(\mathbf{v}) \geq 0</m> for all <m>\mathbf{v} \neq \mathbf{0}</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>A</m> is <term>negative definite</term> if
                        <m>q(\mathbf{v}) \lt 0</m> for all <m>\mathbf{v} \neq \mathbf{0}</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>A</m> is <term>negative semidefinite</term> if
                        <m>q(\mathbf{v}) \leq 0</m> for all <m>\mathbf{v} \neq \mathbf{0}</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>A</m> is <term>indefinite</term> if <m>q</m> takes both
                        positive and negative values.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <example xml:id="ex-definiteness-test">
        <title>Testing Definiteness by Inspection</title>
        <p>
            We test the definiteness of three quadratic forms.
        </p>
        <p>
            (a) <m>q_A(\mathbf{x}) = x_1^2 + 4x_1 x_2 + 3x_2^2</m>.
            We evaluate at two points:
            <me>
                q_A(-1, -2) = 1 + 8 + 12 = 21 \gt 0,
                \qquad
                q_A(-3, 2) = 9 - 24 + 12 = -3 \lt 0.
            </me>
            Since <m>q_A</m> takes both positive and negative values, it is
            <em>indefinite</em>.
        </p>
        <p>
            (b) <m>q_B(\mathbf{x}) = 3x_1^2 + 7x_2^2</m>.
            Since <m>x_1^2, x_2^2 \geq 0</m>, the sum is always non-negative.
            It is zero only when <m>x_1 = x_2 = 0</m>. Therefore <m>q_B</m> is
            <em>positive definite</em>.
        </p>
        <p>
            (c) <m>q_D(\mathbf{x}) = x_1^2</m>.
            Here <m>q_D(0, 1) = 0</m> even though
            <m>(0, 1)^\top \neq \mathbf{0}</m>. Therefore <m>q_D</m> is
            <em>positive semidefinite</em>.
        </p>
    </example>

    <p>
        Testing definiteness by trying specific values (as above) is fine for disproving
        positive definiteness, but not for proving it. The following two theorems give
        systematic criteria.
    </p>

    <example xml:id="ex-diagonal-quadratic-forms">
        <title>Diagonal Quadratic Forms as Model Cases</title>
        <p>
            For a diagonal matrix
            <m>D=\operatorname{diag}(d_1,d_2)</m>, the quadratic form is
            <me>
                q_D(\mathbf{x})=d_1x_1^2+d_2x_2^2.
            </me>
            In this case, the sign pattern of the coefficients already reveals the geometry.
        </p>
        <p>
            (a) For
            <m>q_1(x_1,x_2)=4x_1^2+x_2^2</m>, both coefficients are positive, so
            <m>q_1(\mathbf{x})\gt 0</m> for every non-zero <m>\mathbf{x}</m>. The level line
            <m>q_1(\mathbf{x})=1</m> is an ellipse.
        </p>
        <image width="30%">
            <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9,>=stealth,line cap=round,line join=round]
    \draw[->,gray!80] (-1.6,0) -- (1.6,0) node[right] {$x_1$};
    \draw[->,gray!80] (0,-1.4) -- (0,1.4) node[above] {$x_2$};
    \draw[blue,thick] (0,0) ellipse [x radius=0.5, y radius=1];
\end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
            <caption>
                The level line <m>4x_1^2+x_2^2=1</m> is an ellipse.
            </caption>
        </image>
        <p>
            (b) For
            <m>q_2(x_1,x_2)=4x_1^2-x_2^2</m>, one coefficient is positive and one is
            negative. Hence the form is indefinite. The level line <m>q_2(\mathbf{x})=1</m>
            is a hyperbola.
        </p>
        <image width="30%">
            <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9,>=stealth,line cap=round,line join=round]
    \draw[->,gray!80] (-2.2,0) -- (2.2,0) node[right] {$x_1$};
    \draw[->,gray!80] (0,-2.1) -- (0,2.1) node[above] {$x_2$};
    \draw[blue,thick,samples=100,domain=0.55:2] plot (\x,{sqrt(4*\x*\x-1)});
    \draw[blue,thick,samples=100,domain=0.55:2] plot (\x,{-sqrt(4*\x*\x-1)});
    \draw[blue,thick,samples=100,domain=-2:-0.55] plot (\x,{sqrt(4*\x*\x-1)});
    \draw[blue,thick,samples=100,domain=-2:-0.55] plot (\x,{-sqrt(4*\x*\x-1)});
\end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
            <caption>
                The level line <m>4x_1^2-x_2^2=1</m> is a hyperbola.
            </caption>
        </image>
        <p>
            (c) For
            <m>q_3(x_1,x_2)=x_1^2</m>, we have <m>q_3(\mathbf{x})\geq 0</m> for all
            <m>\mathbf{x}</m>, but <m>q_3(0,1)=0</m>. So the form is positive semidefinite.
            The level set <m>q_3(\mathbf{x})=1</m> consists of two vertical lines.
        </p>
        <image width="30%">
            <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9,>=stealth,line cap=round,line join=round]
    \draw[->,gray!80] (-2.2,0) -- (2.2,0) node[right] {$x_1$};
    \draw[->,gray!80] (0,-1.8) -- (0,1.8) node[above] {$x_2$};
    \draw[blue,thick] (-1,-1.6) -- (-1,1.6);
    \draw[blue,thick] (1,-1.6) -- (1,1.6);
    \draw[blue,dashed] (0,-1.6) -- (0,1.6);
\end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
            <caption>
                For <m>x_1^2=1</m>, the level set consists of the lines <m>x_1=\pm 1</m>; the dashed line <m>x_1=0</m> corresponds to the zero level.
            </caption>
        </image>
        <p>
            These diagonal examples motivate the general criterion below: once a symmetric
            matrix is orthogonally diagonalized, its quadratic form reduces to exactly this
            kind of expression.
        </p>
    </example>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-definiteness-eigenvalues">
        <title>Definiteness Criterion via Eigenvalues</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>A</m> be a symmetric <m>n \times n</m> matrix with eigenvalues
                <m>\lambda_1, \ldots, \lambda_n</m> (all real, by
                <xref ref="thm-symmetric-diagonalizable"/>).
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>A</m> is positive definite (resp. semidefinite) if and only if
                        all eigenvalues satisfy <m>\lambda_i \gt 0</m>
                        (resp. <m>\lambda_i \geq 0</m>).
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>A</m> is negative definite (resp. semidefinite) if and only if
                        all eigenvalues satisfy <m>\lambda_i \lt 0</m>
                        (resp. <m>\lambda_i \leq 0</m>).
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>A</m> is indefinite if and only if there exist both a positive
                        and a negative eigenvalue.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </statement>
        <proof>
            <p>
                By <xref ref="thm-symmetric-diagonalizable"/>, there is an orthogonal matrix
                <m>P</m> and a diagonal matrix
                <m>D=\operatorname{diag}(\lambda_1,\ldots,\lambda_n)</m> such that
                <m>A=PDP^\top</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                Now take any vector <m>\mathbf{x}</m> and write it in the form
                <m>\mathbf{x}=P\mathbf{y}</m>. Then
                <me>
                    \mathbf{x}^\top A\mathbf{x}
                    =
                    (P\mathbf{y})^\top A(P\mathbf{y})
                    =
                    \mathbf{y}^\top(P^\top A P)\mathbf{y}
                    =
                    \mathbf{y}^\top D\mathbf{y}
                    =
                    \lambda_1 y_1^2+\cdots+\lambda_n y_n^2.
                </me>
                So the sign of the quadratic form is determined entirely by the signs of the
                eigenvalues.
            </p>
            <p>
                If all eigenvalues are positive, then every term on the right-hand side is
                non-negative and at least one is positive whenever <m>\mathbf{y}\neq\mathbf{0}</m>,
                so the quadratic form is positive definite. The semidefinite, negative
                definite, and negative semidefinite cases are analogous. If there are both
                positive and negative eigenvalues, then choosing <m>\mathbf{y}</m> along the
                corresponding coordinate axes produces both positive and negative values, so
                the form is indefinite.
            </p>
        </proof>
    </theorem>

    <example xml:id="ex-eigenvalue-criterion-positive-definite">
        <title>Positive Definiteness from Eigenvalues</title>
        <p>
            Consider
            <me>
                A=\begin{pmatrix}4 &amp; 1\\ 1 &amp; 4\end{pmatrix}.
            </me>
            Its characteristic polynomial is
            <me>
                p_A(\lambda)
                =
                \det\begin{pmatrix}4-\lambda &amp; 1\\ 1 &amp; 4-\lambda\end{pmatrix}
                =
                (4-\lambda)^2-1
                =
                (\lambda-5)(\lambda-3).
            </me>
            So the eigenvalues are <m>5</m> and <m>3</m>, both positive. By the
            eigenvalue criterion, <m>A</m> is positive definite.
        </p>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ex-eigenvalue-criterion-indefinite">
        <title>Indefiniteness from Mixed Eigenvalue Signs</title>
        <p>
            Consider
            <me>
                B=\begin{pmatrix}1 &amp; 2\\ 2 &amp; 1\end{pmatrix}.
            </me>
            We compute
            <me>
                p_B(\lambda)
                =
                \det\begin{pmatrix}1-\lambda &amp; 2\\ 2 &amp; 1-\lambda\end{pmatrix}
                =
                (1-\lambda)^2-4
                =
                (\lambda-3)(\lambda+1).
            </me>
            Thus the eigenvalues are <m>3</m> and <m>-1</m>. Since there is one positive
            and one negative eigenvalue, the matrix <m>B</m> is indefinite.
        </p>
        <p>
            This agrees with direct values of the quadratic form:
            <me>
                q_B(1,1)=6\gt 0,
                \qquad
                q_B(1,-1)=-2\lt 0.
            </me>
        </p>
    </example>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-sylvester-criterion">
        <title>Sylvester's Criterion</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>A</m> be a symmetric <m>n \times n</m> matrix. Denote by <m>A_i</m>
                the <m>i \times i</m> leading principal submatrix (the submatrix formed by
                the first <m>i</m> rows and columns of <m>A</m>).
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>A</m> is <em>positive definite</em> if and only if
                        <me>
                            \det(A_i) \gt 0 \quad \text{for all } i \in \{1, \ldots, n\}.
                        </me>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>A</m> is <em>negative definite</em> if and only if
                        <me>
                            (-1)^i \det(A_i) \gt 0 \quad \text{for all } i \in \{1, \ldots, n\}.
                        </me>
                        In other words, the leading principal minors alternate in sign:
                        <m>\det(A_1) \lt 0</m>, <m>\det(A_2) \gt 0</m>,
                        <m>\det(A_3) \lt 0</m>, and so on.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

    <image width="60%">
        <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.95, line cap=round, line join=round]
    \fill[green!10] (0,1) rectangle (3,4);
    \fill[blue!10] (0,2) rectangle (2,4);
    \fill[red!12] (0,3) rectangle (1,4);
    \draw[gray!70] (0,0) grid (4,4);
    \draw[very thick,green!60!black] (0,1) rectangle (3,4);
    \draw[very thick,blue!70!black] (0,2) rectangle (2,4);
    \draw[very thick,red!70!black] (0,3) rectangle (1,4);
    \node at (0.5,3.5) {$a_{11}$};
    \node at (1.5,3.5) {$a_{12}$};
    \node at (2.5,3.5) {$a_{13}$};
    \node at (3.5,3.5) {$a_{14}$};
    \node at (0.5,2.5) {$a_{21}$};
    \node at (1.5,2.5) {$a_{22}$};
    \node at (2.5,2.5) {$a_{23}$};
    \node at (3.5,2.5) {$a_{24}$};
    \node at (0.5,1.5) {$a_{31}$};
    \node at (1.5,1.5) {$a_{32}$};
    \node at (2.5,1.5) {$a_{33}$};
    \node at (3.5,1.5) {$a_{34}$};
    \node at (0.5,0.5) {$a_{41}$};
    \node at (1.5,0.5) {$a_{42}$};
    \node at (2.5,0.5) {$a_{43}$};
    \node at (3.5,0.5) {$a_{44}$};
    \node[red!70!black,right] at (4.05,3.5) {$A_1$};
    \node[blue!70!black,right] at (4.05,2.5) {$A_2$};
    \node[green!50!black,right] at (4.05,1.5) {$A_3$};
\end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
        <caption>
            Leading principal submatrices are the successive top-left blocks of the matrix:
            first <m>A_1</m>, then <m>A_2</m>, then <m>A_3</m>, and so on.
        </caption>
    </image>

    <example xml:id="ex-sylvester-positive-definite">
        <title>Positive Definiteness via Sylvester's Criterion</title>
        <p>
            Consider
            <me>
                A=\begin{pmatrix}2 &amp; 1\\ 1 &amp; 3\end{pmatrix}.
            </me>
            The leading principal submatrices are
            <me>
                A_1=\begin{pmatrix}2\end{pmatrix},
                \qquad
                A_2=A.
            </me>
            Their determinants are
            <me>
                \det(A_1)=2\gt 0,
                \qquad
                \det(A_2)=2\cdot 3-1\cdot 1=5\gt 0.
            </me>
            Since all leading principal minors are positive, <m>A</m> is positive definite.
        </p>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ex-sylvester-negative-definite">
        <title>Negative Definiteness via Sylvester's Criterion</title>
        <p>
            Consider
            <me>
                B=\begin{pmatrix}-2 &amp; 1\\ 1 &amp; -3\end{pmatrix}.
            </me>
            We compute
            <me>
                \det(B_1)=-2\lt 0,
                \qquad
                \det(B)=(-2)(-3)-1=5\gt 0.
            </me>
            The leading principal minors alternate in sign, so Sylvester's criterion shows
            that <m>B</m> is negative definite.
        </p>
    </example>

    <!-- AUTHOR NOTE: A full proof of Sylvester's criterion is omitted here because it relies on a longer inductive determinant argument than we want to develop in this first pass through the topic. -->
    <!-- ADDED: explain how students should use Sylvester's criterion -->
    <remark>
        <p>
            In practice, Sylvester's criterion is most useful when the eigenvalues are
            inconvenient to compute exactly. For a symmetric matrix, it gives a fast test
            for positive or negative definiteness using only determinants of smaller
            submatrices.
        </p>
    </remark>

    <remark xml:id="rem-local-extrema-forward">
        <title>Connection to Local Extrema</title>
        <p>
            Quadratic forms play a central role in the analysis of local extrema of
            functions <m>f \colon \R^n \to \R</m>.
            Near a critical point <m>\mathbf{x}_0</m> (where
            <m>\operatorname{grad} f(\mathbf{x}_0) = \mathbf{0}</m>),
            the Taylor expansion gives
            <me>
                f(\mathbf{x})
                \approx f(\mathbf{x}_0)
                + \tfrac{1}{2}(\mathbf{x} - \mathbf{x}_0)^\top
                  \operatorname{Hess} f(\mathbf{x}_0)
                  (\mathbf{x} - \mathbf{x}_0).
            </me>
            The second-order term is a quadratic form with the Hessian matrix
            <m>\operatorname{Hess} f(\mathbf{x}_0)</m>:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    If <m>\operatorname{Hess} f(\mathbf{x}_0)</m> is positive definite,
                    then <m>\mathbf{x}_0</m> is a strict local minimum.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    If <m>\operatorname{Hess} f(\mathbf{x}_0)</m> is negative definite,
                    then <m>\mathbf{x}_0</m> is a strict local maximum.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    If <m>\operatorname{Hess} f(\mathbf{x}_0)</m> is indefinite,
                    then <m>\mathbf{x}_0</m> is a saddle point.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            This connection is developed in detail in Section 2.2
            (Error Propagation, Taylor Expansion, and Extrema).
        </p>
    </remark>

    <remark xml:id="rem-principal-axes">
        <title>Principal Axes and the Geometry of Quadratic Forms</title>
        <p>
            Since the matrix <m>A</m> of a quadratic form is symmetric, it can be
            diagonalized by an orthogonal matrix: <m>A = PDP^\top</m>.
            Here we only use the basic idea of changing coordinates; a full general theory
            of change of basis is not needed yet.
            Substituting the coordinate change <m>\mathbf{x} = P\mathbf{y}</m>:
            <me>
                q(\mathbf{x})
                = \mathbf{x}^\top A \mathbf{x}
                = (P\mathbf{y})^\top A (P\mathbf{y})
                = \mathbf{y}^\top (P^\top A P) \mathbf{y}
                = \mathbf{y}^\top D \mathbf{y}
                = \lambda_1 y_1^2 + \cdots + \lambda_n y_n^2.
            </me>
            In the new coordinates <m>\mathbf{y}</m>, the quadratic form has
            <em>no mixed terms</em>. The columns of <m>P</m> (the eigenvectors of <m>A</m>)
            define the <term>principal axes</term> of the quadratic form.
        </p>
        <p>
            Geometrically, the level sets <m>\{\mathbf{x} \mid q(\mathbf{x}) = c\}</m>
            are ellipses (positive definite case), hyperbolas (indefinite case), or
            degenerate shapes in between. The principal axes are exactly the axes of
            symmetry of these level curves.
        </p>
        <image width="45%">
            <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.85,>=stealth,line cap=round,line join=round]
    \draw[->,gray!80] (-3,0) -- (3,0) node[right] {$x_1$};
    \draw[->,gray!80] (0,-3) -- (0,3) node[above] {$x_2$};
    \begin{scope}[rotate=30]
        \draw[blue,thick] (0,0) ellipse [x radius=2.2, y radius=1.1];
        \draw[red,dashed,thick,->] (-2.7,0) -- (2.7,0) node[right] {$y_1$};
        \draw[red,dashed,thick,->] (0,-1.8) -- (0,1.8) node[above] {$y_2$};
    \end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
            <caption>
                After an orthogonal change of coordinates, the mixed term disappears and the level curve aligns with the principal axes <m>y_1</m> and <m>y_2</m>.
            </caption>
        </image>
    </remark>

    <exercises xml:id="exercises-quadratic-forms">
        <!-- ADDED: checkpoint title for consistency with the rest of the chapter -->
        <title>Check Your Understanding</title>

        <exercise xml:id="exer-expand-quadratic-form">
            <title>Expand a Quadratic Form</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let
                    <me>
                        A = \begin{pmatrix} 2 &amp; -1 \\ -1 &amp; 3 \end{pmatrix}.
                    </me>
                    Write out the quadratic form
                    <m>q_A(\mathbf{x}) = \mathbf{x}^\top A \mathbf{x}</m>
                    explicitly as a polynomial in <m>x_1, x_2</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <choices>
                <choice>
                    <statement><p><m>2x_1^2- x_1x_2 + 3x_2^2</m></p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>Incorrect. The mixed term receives contributions from both off-diagonal entries.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice correct="yes">
                    <statement><p><m>2x_1^2-2x_1x_2+3x_2^2</m></p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>Correct. The mixed coefficient is <m>a_{12}+a_{21}=-1+(-1)=-2</m>.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice>
                    <statement><p><m>2x_1^2+x_1x_2+3x_2^2</m></p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>Incorrect. The sign of the mixed term is negative, not positive.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice>
                    <statement><p><m>2x_1^2-2x_2^2+3x_1x_2</m></p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>Incorrect. The squared terms and mixed term have been combined incorrectly.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
            </choices>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="exer-definiteness-eigenvalues">
            <title>Classify by Eigenvalues</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Determine the eigenvalues of
                    <me>
                        A = \begin{pmatrix} 3 &amp; 1 \\ 1 &amp; 3 \end{pmatrix}
                    </me>
                    and use them to decide whether <m>A</m> is positive definite,
                    negative definite, or indefinite.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <choices>
                <choice>
                    <statement><p>Negative definite</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>Incorrect. Both eigenvalues are positive, not negative.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice correct="yes">
                    <statement><p>Positive definite</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>Correct. The eigenvalues are <m>4</m> and <m>2</m>, so both are positive.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice>
                    <statement><p>Indefinite</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>Incorrect. An indefinite matrix needs both a positive and a negative eigenvalue.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice>
                    <statement><p>Negative semidefinite</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>Incorrect. The matrix has strictly positive eigenvalues.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
            </choices>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="exer-sylvester-3x3">
            <title>Sylvester's Criterion for a <m>3\times 3</m> Matrix</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Apply Sylvester's criterion to determine whether
                    <me>
                        A = \begin{pmatrix} 2 &amp; 1 &amp; 0 \\ 1 &amp; 3 &amp; 1 \\ 0 &amp; 1 &amp; 4 \end{pmatrix}
                    </me>
                    is positive definite.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
                <p>
                    Compute <m>\det(A_1)</m>, <m>\det(A_2)</m>, and <m>\det(A_3)</m> in turn.
                </p>
            </hint>
            <!-- ADDED: worked solution for the longer checkpoint exercise -->
            <solution>
                <p>
                    The leading principal submatrices are
                    <me>
                        A_1=\begin{pmatrix}2\end{pmatrix},
                        \qquad
                        A_2=\begin{pmatrix}2 &amp; 1\\ 1 &amp; 3\end{pmatrix},
                        \qquad
                        A_3=A.
                    </me>
                </p>
                <p>
                    Their determinants are
                    <me>
                        \det(A_1)=2,
                        \qquad
                        \det(A_2)=2\cdot 3-1\cdot 1=5,
                    </me>
                    and
                    <me>
                        \det(A_3)
                        =
                        2\det\begin{pmatrix}3 &amp; 1\\ 1 &amp; 4\end{pmatrix}
                        -1\det\begin{pmatrix}1 &amp; 1\\ 0 &amp; 4\end{pmatrix}
                        =2(12-1)-4
                        =18.
                    </me>
                </p>
                <p>
                    All three leading principal minors are positive. By Sylvester's criterion,
                    the matrix is positive definite.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="exer-hessian-classification">
            <title>Classify a Critical Point</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The function <m>f \colon \R^2 \to \R</m> has a critical
                    point at the origin with Hessian
                    <me>
                        \operatorname{Hess} f(\mathbf{0})
                        = \begin{pmatrix} 4 &amp; -2 \\ -2 &amp; 1 \end{pmatrix}.
                    </me>
                    Is the origin a local minimum, a local maximum, or a saddle point?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
                <p>
                    Use Sylvester's criterion or compute the eigenvalues of the Hessian.
                </p>
            </hint>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    Let
                    <me>
                        H=\begin{pmatrix}4 &amp; -2\\ -2 &amp; 1\end{pmatrix}.
                    </me>
                    We apply Sylvester's criterion. The first leading principal minor is
                    <m>\det(H_1)=4\gt 0</m>, while
                    <me>
                        \det(H)=4\cdot 1-(-2)(-2)=4-4=0.
                    </me>
                </p>
                <p>
                    So <m>H</m> is not positive definite and not negative definite. In fact,
                    it is positive semidefinite. Therefore the second-derivative test is
                    inconclusive here: from the Hessian alone we cannot decide whether the
                    origin is a local minimum, a local maximum, or a saddle point.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

</subsection>
